pass `ENVIRONMENT=production` directly from the command line when starting Uvicorn. However, the syntax differs between Windows and Linux/macOS.

## 1. Windows PowerShell

```
$env:ENVIRONMENT="production"
$env:GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT="workspace-prod-511311"
$env:DEPLOY_TYPE="app-engine"

uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

## 2. Windows CMD

```
set ENVIRONMENT=production
set GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=workspace-prod-511311
set DEPLOY_TYPE=app-engine

uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

## 3. Linux / macOS

```
ENVIRONMENT=production \
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=workspace-prod-511311 \
DEPLOY_TYPE=app-engine \
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

## 4. Read the values in Python

Your existing code will work with these environment variables:

```
import os

environment = os.getenv("ENVIRONMENT", "development").lower()
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT = os.getenv("GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT")
DEPLOY_TYPE = os.getenv("DEPLOY_TYPE")

print(f"Environment: {environment}")
print(f"GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT: {GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT}")
print(f"DEPLOY_TYPE: {DEPLOY_TYPE}")
```

Expected output:

```
Environment: production
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT: workspace-prod-511311
DEPLOY_TYPE: app-engine
```

## 5. Recommended for your Weather Agent

If you want to pass the environment in a single command on Windows PowerShell, use:

```
$env:ENVIRONMENT="production"; uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

Note: `--reload` is intended for development. For production deployment to App Engine, run the application using your production server configuration without `--reload`.

Also, if you set environment variables in the command line, your `os.getenv()` calls will take precedence over fallback values loaded from `.env`.